ef8e4da89b22ef7973d0d41beedfa2b42f7bb272. Copyright Amazon.com, Inc. or its affiliates.
Licensed under MIT-0. `app/` began as a copy of it, its licence and notices are kept in
`app/LICENSE`, and the Calm Frames work sits alongside it under Apache-2.0.

**Shaka Player**, https://github.com/shaka-project/shaka-player, Apache-2.0. Bundled by the
sample app's own build. Its licence text is in `app/LICENSE-THIRD-PARTY`.

**Inter**, version 4.001, Copyright 2016 The Inter Project Authors, https://github.com/rsms/inter,
SIL Open Font License 1.1. `app/assets/fonts/` holds three static instances made from the
variable font and renamed to their file names, which the licence permits because it reserves no
font name; `docs/design/assets/` holds the variable font. The licence text travels with both, as
`app/assets/fonts/LICENSE-Inter` and `docs/design/assets/Inter-LICENSE.txt`.

**A caveat on the sample's own notices.** The sample's root licence is MIT-0 and that is what
`app/LICENSE` carries. Two things in it say otherwise on their own first lines:
`app/src/w3cmedia/shakaplayer/ShakaPlayer.ts` and thirteen source files inside
`app/shaka-setup/shaka-rel-v4.8.5-r1.2.tar.gz` are marked "AMAZON PROPRIETARY/CONFIDENTIAL" and
refer to an accompanying LICENSE.TXT that is in neither the archive nor the upstream repository,
where its URL answers 404. Both are redistributed here exactly as Amazon publishes them in the
public sample, with every notice kept. `docs/AMAZON_SAMPLE_LICENSE_REVIEW.md` records what was
checked and holds a clarification request for Amazon. Until an answer arrives, the MIT-0 grant on
the repository they come from is the only licence statement we can point to.

